   114	//    has doubled. Consequence: a producer deleter, capsule destructor or __dlpack__ that never returns makes interpreter
   115	//    exit wait for it (the producer's contract violation; waiting is the honest outcome, an early return aborts).
   116	//  * GIL: the waiting thread releases the GIL with PyEval_SaveThread (an admitted callback that needs the GIL can then
   117	//    finish), waits on the condition variable with no Python object touched, drops the mutex, and only then re-takes
   118	//    the GIL with PyEval_RestoreThread, called explicitly in a NON-noexcept frame (not from a destructor such as
   119	//    py::gil_scoped_release's): if CPython exits a waiting daemon thread there during finalization, the forced unwind
   120	//    travels through ordinary frames to pybind11's dispatcher, which rethrows abi::__forced_unwind (pybind11 v2.12
   121	//    pybind11.h:1037), instead of meeting a noexcept boundary. No thread waits for the GIL while holding the mutex.
   122	//  * P1T-FIX6, round-6 review finding V6-01 (MAJOR): FIX5 also made synchronize() (any thread) wait, without closing,
   123	//    for releases already admitted on other threads. That wait deadlocked: thread A holds an application lock (a
   124	//    producer's allocator lock) and calls synchronize(); thread B is releasing a refused operand whose finite producer
   125	//    deleter needs that lock; A waits for B and B waits for A (releasing the GIL does not release A's lock; reviewer
   126	//    probe sync_lock_inversion_cpu.py, rc 1 on acd42fa). The wait is REMOVED: synchronize() never waits for another
   127	//    thread. A release that another thread popped off the pending list before this call is that thread's release:
   128	//    it finishes there (it was handed to that thread's call, not to this one), and synchronize() reports and releases
   129	//    only what is still on the list. The only waiter is the exit hook (close_admission_and_wait), which is where the
   130	//    wait is needed: there, finalization must not start under a running callback (V5-01). The exit hook's wait is
   131	//    documented (tc_signal/__init__.py, README): it waits for callbacks admitted before it closed admission, so the
   132	//    exiting thread must not hold a lock such a callback needs (CPython's own exit has the same rule for the
   133	//    non-daemon threads it joins, threading._shutdown).
   134	//    Prior art: Coffman, Elphick & Shoshani (1971) "System Deadlocks" (circular wait is one of the four necessary
   135	//    conditions; removing the wait from ordinary calls removes the cycle); lock-ordering discipline (never block on
   136	//    another thread's callback while the caller may hold locks it needs); RCU (McKenney & Slingwine 1998:
   137	//    synchronize_rcu waits only for readers that began before it) is why a separate "wait for releases" API would need a
   138	//    pre-call generation boundary and a no-locks-held contract; none is offered (no caller needs it).
   139	// The pending list itself is unchanged (still GIL-protected; nothing here holds a reference into it across a wait).
   140	//
   141	// Prior art: core's own ImportReleaseLifecycle (tensor_cuda/src/bindings.cpp, P1T-FIX2 N5 and P1T-FIX3 V3-01). Reused
   142	// from it: the closing flag + in-flight count + condition variable, the unbounded wait with the GIL released and a
   143	// doubling stderr diagnostic schedule, the thread-local depth for self-exclusion, "leak, never enter Python, after
   144	// closing", and the atexit-before-finalizing argument. Different here: the add-on's releasers already hold the GIL (no
   145	// PyGILState_Ensure), so admission is two seq_cst atomics instead of a mutex section; two section kinds (releases and
   146	// producer callbacks); the GIL is re-taken explicitly rather than by a destructor; only the exit hook waits. Further
   147	// prior art: CPython's threading._shutdown / wait_for_thread_shutdown (finalization waits, unbounded, for non-daemon
   148	// threads already started); RCU grace periods (synchronize_rcu waits for every reader that entered before it, while new
   149	// readers do not delay it; McKenney & Slingwine 1998) and the Linux percpu_ref "kill, then wait for zero"; the
   150	// store-buffering (Dekker, Dijkstra 1965) handshake for flag + counter admission; glibc/libstdc++ forced unwinding
   151	// (abi::__forced_unwind must be rethrown by a catch-all; a noexcept frame terminates). Ours: applying the protocol to the
   152	// add-on's deleter, capsule-drop and producer-callback sites. Unverified here (lead to check): "CPython 3.12
   153	// take_gil _PyThreadState_MustExit PyThread_exit_thread", "synchronize_rcu McKenney Slingwine 1998".
   154	namespace {
   155	
   156	enum LifecycleKind : int { kRelease = 0, kCallback = 1, kKinds = 2 };
   157	
   158	struct Lifecycle {
   159	  std::mutex mu;                           // only for the waiter's condition variable; admission never takes it
   160	  std::condition_variable cv;
   161	  std::atomic<bool> closing{false};
   162	  std::atomic<long long> in_flight[kKinds];
   163	  Lifecycle() {
   164	    for (auto& c : in_flight) c.store(0);
   165	  }
   166	};
   167	
   168	// Never destroyed (a callback may run during static destruction). Constructed at module import (init_pending_state).
   169	Lifecycle& lifecycle() {
   170	  static Lifecycle* s = new Lifecycle();
   171	  return *s;
   172	}
   173	
   174	// Admitted sections on THIS thread's stack, per kind (self-exclusion; also synchronize()'s skip rule).
   175	thread_local long long t_depth[kKinds] = {0, 0};
   176	
   177	// Static storage: counting never allocates.
   178	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_admitted[kKinds];
   179	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_deleters_leaked_after_close{0};   // V5-01: "a late release leaks, and is counted"
   180	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_deleters_leaked_finalizing{0};
   181	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_capsules_kept_after_close{0};
   182	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_capsules_kept_finalizing{0};
   183	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_callbacks_refused{0};
   184	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_lifecycle_lock_failures{0};
   185	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_hook_calls{0};
   186	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_in_flight_at_close{0};
   187	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_wait_us{0};
   188	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_wait_diagnostics{0};
   189	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_wait_overruns{0};
   190	// P1T-FIX6 (V6-01): FIX5's g_sync_waits / g_sync_wait_us / g_sync_wait_diagnostics counted synchronize()'s wait for
   191	// other threads; that wait is removed, and so are they (and their pending_stats keys).
   192	
   193	bool interpreter_accepts_python() noexcept {
   194	  if (!Py_IsInitialized()) return false;
   195	#if PY_VERSION_HEX >= 0x030D0000
   196	  if (Py_IsFinalizing()) return false;
   197	#else
   198	  if (_Py_IsFinalizing()) return false;
   199	#endif
   200	  return true;
   201	}
   202	
   203	enum class Admission { kAdmitted, kClosed, kFinalizing };
   204	
   205	void leave_count(Lifecycle& lc, LifecycleKind k) noexcept {
   206	  lc.in_flight[k].fetch_sub(1, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
   207	  // Take and drop the mutex before notifying: a waiter checks its predicate while holding it, so this leave either
   208	  // happened before that check (seen) or waits until the waiter blocks (then the notify wakes it). No lost wakeup.
   209	  try {
   210	    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(lc.mu);
   211	  } catch (const std::system_error&) {
   212	    g_lifecycle_lock_failures.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);  // never observed; the waiter also polls
   213	  }
   214	  lc.cv.notify_all();
   215	}
   216	
   217	Admission admit(LifecycleKind k) noexcept {
   218	  if (!interpreter_accepts_python()) return Admission::kFinalizing;
   219	  Lifecycle& lc = lifecycle();
   220	  lc.in_flight[k].fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
   221	  if (lc.closing.load(std::memory_order_seq_cst)) {
   222	    leave_count(lc, k);  // back out: admission closed
   223	    return Admission::kClosed;
   224	  }
   225	  ++t_depth[k];
   226	  g_admitted[k].fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   227	  return Admission::kAdmitted;
   228	}
   229	
   230	void leave(LifecycleKind k) noexcept {
   231	  --t_depth[k];
   232	  leave_count(lifecycle(), k);
   233	}
   234	
   235	// RAII for the producer-callback section of to_capsule. Throws RuntimeError (before any callback) when refused.
   236	class CallbackSection {
   237	 public:
   238	  explicit CallbackSection(const char* role) {
   239	    const Admission a = admit(kCallback);
   240	    if (a == Admission::kAdmitted) return;
   241	    g_callbacks_refused.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   242	    throw std::runtime_error(std::string("tc_signal: operand '") + role + "': " +
   243	                             (a == Admission::kClosed
   244	                                  ? "interpreter exit has begun (tc_signal's exit hook closed admission)"
   245	                                  : "the interpreter is finalizing") +
   246	                             ", so the producer's __dlpack__ is not called; refused before acquiring anything");
   247	  }
   248	  ~CallbackSection() { leave(kCallback); }
   249	  CallbackSection(const CallbackSection&) = delete;
   250	  CallbackSection& operator=(const CallbackSection&) = delete;
   251	};
   252	
   253	struct WaitOutcome {
   254	  long long running_at_start = 0;  // admitted sections of OTHER threads when the wait began
   255	  long long own[kKinds] = {0, 0};  // this thread's own (excluded)
   256	  long long waited_us = 0;
   257	  unsigned long long diagnostics = 0;
   258	  bool overran = false;            // outlasted the first-diagnostic delay
   259	};
   260	
   261	// Admitted sections (releases and producer callbacks) of OTHER threads: the calling thread's own are excluded.
   262	long long others_running(const Lifecycle& lc, const long long own[kKinds]) noexcept {
   263	  return lc.in_flight[kRelease].load(std::memory_order_seq_cst) - own[kRelease] +
   264	         lc.in_flight[kCallback].load(std::memory_order_seq_cst) - own[kCallback];
   265	}
   266	
   267	// The part of the exit wait that runs with the GIL released: the mutex and condition variable only, no Python object.
   268	void wait_without_gil(Lifecycle& lc, double first_diag_s, std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point t0,
   269	                      WaitOutcome& w) noexcept {
   270	  using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
   271	  const auto first = std::chrono::duration_cast<clock::duration>(std::chrono::duration<double>(first_diag_s));
   272	  auto quiet = [&lc, &w] { return others_running(lc, w.own) <= 0; };
   273	  try {
   274	    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(lc.mu);
   275	    auto next = t0 + first;
   276	    while (!lc.cv.wait_until(lk, next, quiet)) {
   277	      // Still running at the diagnostic time: report, then keep waiting (never return early: V3-01, V5-01).
   278	      w.overran = true;
   279	      const long long running = others_running(lc, w.own);
   280	      lk.unlock();  // a blocked stderr must not stop a callback from leaving
   281	      const auto now = clock::now();
   282	      std::fprintf(stderr,
   283	                   "tc_signal: %lld DLPack producer callback(s) (deleter, capsule destructor or __dlpack__) admitted "
   284	                   "on other threads before interpreter exit still running after %.1f s; interpreter exit waits for "
   285	                   "them (no new one is admitted)\n",
   286	                   running, std::chrono::duration<double>(now - t0).count());
   287	      std::fflush(stderr);
   288	      ++w.diagnostics;
   289	      const auto waited = now - t0;  // next line when the time waited has doubled (at least one tick: no busy loop)
   290	      const auto tick = clock::duration(1);
   291	      next = now + (waited > tick ? waited : tick);
   292	      lk.lock();
   293	    }
   294	  } catch (const std::system_error&) {
   295	    // A mutex / condition-variable failure (never observed): keep waiting by polling, never return early.
   296	    g_lifecycle_lock_failures.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   297	    while (!quiet()) std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(tuning::kLifecyclePollMs));
   298	  }
   299	}
   300	
   301	// The exit hook's wait (P1T-FIX6: its only caller; ordinary calls never wait for another thread, V6-01): until no
   302	// admitted section of another thread is running. The GIL is released for the wait and re-taken explicitly here (see
   303	// the design block above for why not by a destructor).
   304	WaitOutcome wait_for_other_threads(double first_diag_s) {
   305	  Lifecycle& lc = lifecycle();
   306	  WaitOutcome w;
   307	  w.own[kRelease] = t_depth[kRelease];
   308	  w.own[kCallback] = t_depth[kCallback];
   309	  w.running_at_start = others_running(lc, w.own);
   310	  if (w.running_at_start <= 0) return w;  // nothing to wait for: the GIL is not touched
   311	  if (!(first_diag_s >= 0.0)) first_diag_s = 0.0;   // NaN or negative: diagnose at once (the wait is the same)
   312	  if (first_diag_s > tuning::kLifecycleMaxDiagDelayS) first_diag_s = tuning::kLifecycleMaxDiagDelayS;
   313	  const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
   314	  const bool held = PyGILState_Check() != 0;
   315	  PyThreadState* ts = held ? PyEval_SaveThread() : nullptr;  // GIL released: an admitted callback can finish
   316	  wait_without_gil(lc, first_diag_s, t0, w);
   317	  if (held) PyEval_RestoreThread(ts);  // the mutex is not held here; may exit a daemon thread during finalization
   318	  w.waited_us = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
   319	  return w;
   320	}
   321	
   322	}  // namespace
   323	
   324	void CapsuleRef::drop() noexcept {
   325	  PyObject* p = p_;
   326	  p_ = nullptr;
   327	  if (p == nullptr) return;
   328	  if (!interpreter_accepts_python()) {
   329	    // Finalizing (for example a daemon thread unwinding after CPython exited it): never touch Python. Kept.
   330	    g_capsules_kept_finalizing.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   331	    return;
   332	  }
   333	  // P1T-FIX6 (sweep (g), user Python run from a native frame): FIX5 admitted only the last reference of an UNCONSUMED
   334	  // capsule. The last reference of a consumed ("used_*") capsule also runs the producer's PyCapsule destructor: per the
   335	  // DLPack Python spec that destructor only returns for a consumed capsule, but it is still producer code (a ctypes or
   336	  // Cython destructor runs Python, and may release and re-take the GIL), run from this native frame. Every
   337	  // last-reference drop of a capsule is therefore admitted (waited for by the exit hook), or kept once admission has
   338	  // closed (a consumed capsule kept at exit holds no producer memory: nothing is lost). A capsule with no destructor,
   339	  // or a drop that is not the last reference, runs no producer code either way.
   340	  if (PyCapsule_CheckExact(p) && Py_REFCNT(p) == 1) {
   341	    // The last reference to a DLPack capsule: dropping it runs the producer's destructor (which, for an unconsumed
   342	    // capsule, calls the producer's deleter). A release: admitted, or kept (never run) once admission has closed.
   343	    const Admission a = admit(kRelease);
   344	    if (a != Admission::kAdmitted) {
   345	      (a == Admission::kClosed ? g_capsules_kept_after_close : g_capsules_kept_finalizing)
   346	          .fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   347	      return;
   348	    }
   349	    Py_DECREF(p);
   350	    leave(kRelease);
   351	    return;
   352	  }
   353	  Py_DECREF(p);
   354	}
   355	
   356	// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ capsule acquisition
   357	CapsuleRef to_capsule(py::handle obj, const char* role, bool writable) {
   358	  if (PyCapsule_CheckExact(obj.ptr())) return CapsuleRef(py::reinterpret_borrow<py::object>(obj));
   359	  // P1T-FIX5 (V5-01 sweep (c)): from here to the return, the producer's Python code may run (and release / re-take the
   360	  // GIL): an admitted section, waited for by the exit hook; refused (RuntimeError, nothing called) after it closed.
   361	  CallbackSection section(role);
   362	  if (!py::hasattr(obj, "__dlpack__"))
   363	    refuse_type(role, "expected a DLPack producer (an object with __dlpack__) or a DLPack capsule, got " +
   364	                          type_name(obj));
   365	  // Array API order: ask the producer's device first. A non-CUDA producer is refused here, before __dlpack__ is
   366	  // called with a CUDA stream argument (CPU producers such as NumPy reject any stream other than None).
   367	  if (py::hasattr(obj, "__dlpack_device__")) {
   368	    int device_type = -1;
   369	    try {
   370	      py::tuple dev = obj.attr("__dlpack_device__")().cast<py::tuple>();
   371	      device_type = dev[0].cast<int>();
   372	    } catch (py::cast_error&) {
   373	      refuse_type(role, "__dlpack_device__() did not return a (device_type, device_id) tuple of ints");
   374	    }
   375	    if (device_type != kDLCUDA) {
   376	      std::ostringstream os;
   377	      os << "device type " << device_type << " is not kDLCUDA (2); tc_signal takes CUDA device memory only";
   378	      refuse_value(role, os.str());
   379	    }
   380	  }
   381	  py::object fn = obj.attr("__dlpack__");
   382	  py::object cap;
   383	  const py::tuple max_version = py::make_tuple(tuning::kDlpackMaxMajor, tuning::kDlpackMaxMinor);
   384	  try {
   385	    // Consumer stream = the legacy default stream (DLPack int 1): the producer orders its pending work before it.
   386	    if (writable)
   387	      cap = fn(py::arg("stream") = tuning::kDlpackLegacyStream, py::arg("max_version") = max_version,
   388	               py::arg("copy") = false);
   389	    else
   390	      cap = fn(py::arg("stream") = tuning::kDlpackLegacyStream, py::arg("max_version") = max_version);
   391	  } catch (py::error_already_set& e) {
   392	    // A producer that predates the 2023 keywords rejects them with TypeError: retry with the stream argument only
   393	    // (the array API standard's documented fallback; NumPy and PyTorch consumers do the same).
   394	    if (!e.matches(PyExc_TypeError)) throw;
   395	    cap = fn(py::arg("stream") = tuning::kDlpackLegacyStream);
   396	  }
   397	  if (!PyCapsule_CheckExact(cap.ptr()))
   398	    refuse_type(role, "__dlpack__ returned " + type_name(cap) + ", not a PyCapsule");
   399	  return CapsuleRef(std::move(cap));
   400	}
   401	
   402	const DLTensor* peek(py::handle capsule, const char* role) {
   403	  if (!PyCapsule_CheckExact(capsule.ptr())) refuse_type(role, "expected a DLPack capsule, got " + type_name(capsule));
   404	  if (capsule_kind(capsule, role)) {
   405	    auto* m = static_cast<DLManagedTensorVersioned*>(capsule_pointer(capsule, kCapsuleVersioned));
   406	    check_version(m, role);
   407	    return &m->dl_tensor;
   408	  }
   409	  auto* m = static_cast<DLManagedTensor*>(capsule_pointer(capsule, kCapsule));
   410	  return &m->dl_tensor;
   411	}
   412	
   413	// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ validation
   414	int64_t validate_tensor(const DLTensor* t, const char* role, int ndim) {
   415	  if (t->device.device_type != kDLCUDA) {
   416	    std::ostringstream os;
   417	    os << "device type " << static_cast<int>(t->device.device_type)
   418	       << " is not kDLCUDA (2); tc_signal takes CUDA device memory only";
   419	    refuse_value(role, os.str());
   420	  }
   421	  if (t->dtype.code != kDLFloat || t->dtype.bits != 32 || t->dtype.lanes != 1) {
   422	    std::ostringstream os;
   423	    os << "dtype (code " << static_cast<int>(t->dtype.code) << ", bits " << static_cast<int>(t->dtype.bits)
   424	       << ", lanes " << t->dtype.lanes
   425	       << ") is not float32; tc_signal computes in fp32 only (expand fp16 weights to fp32 first)";
   426	    refuse_type(role, os.str());
   427	  }
   428	  // F03 (P1T-FIX1): a negative rank or a NULL shape array (with rank > 0) is refused before the shape is read.
   429	  if (t->ndim < 0) {
   430	    std::ostringstream os;
   431	    os << "negative number of dimensions " << t->ndim;
   432	    refuse_value(role, os.str());
   433	  }
   434	  if (t->ndim > 0 && t->shape == nullptr) refuse_value(role, "NULL shape array for a tensor with dimensions");
   435	  if (t->ndim != ndim) {
   436	    std::ostringstream os;
   437	    os << "expected " << ndim << " dimensions, got " << t->ndim;
   438	    refuse_value(role, os.str());
   439	  }
   440	  // F03: checked element count and fp32 byte total, before the stride walk below multiplies extents and before any
   441	  // later byte range is formed.
   442	  // P1T-FIX2, review finding N2 (zero-extent rule, checked_math.h ck_extent_bound): EVERY extent is multiplied (a zero
   443	  // or negative one counted as 1) BEFORE the empty-operand refusal, so a shape such as [0, 2^62, 4] is refused as an
   444	  // overflow, never waved through because its count is 0. Prior art: NumPy's array constructor rule (see
   445	  // checked_math.h); the checked builtins (GCC 5, 2015).
   446	  int64_t numel = 1;
   447	  for (int i = 0; i < t->ndim; ++i) {
   448	    if (__builtin_mul_overflow(numel, t->shape[i] > 1 ? t->shape[i] : int64_t{1}, &numel))
   449	      refuse_value(role, "element count of shape " + shape_str(shape_of(t)) +
   450	                             " overflows int64 (every nonzero extent counts, also next to a zero extent)");
   451	  }
   452	  int64_t nbytes = 0;
   453	  if (__builtin_mul_overflow(numel, static_cast<int64_t>(sizeof(float)), &nbytes))
   454	    refuse_value(role, "byte size of shape " + shape_str(shape_of(t)) + " (fp32) overflows int64");
   455	  for (int i = 0; i < t->ndim; ++i)
   456	    if (t->shape[i] <= 0) refuse_value(role, "empty operand (shape " + shape_str(shape_of(t)) + ")");
   457	  // Every extent is >= 1 from here: numel is the element count and nbytes its fp32 byte size.
   458	  if (t->strides != nullptr) {
   459	    // C-contiguous: every dimension of extent > 1 has the compact stride; size-1 dimensions may carry any stride
   460	    // (they are never stepped), as the order and the DLPack/array API conventions allow.
   461	    int64_t want = 1;
   462	    for (int i = t->ndim - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
   463	      if (t->shape[i] != 1 && t->strides[i] != want) {
   464	        std::ostringstream os;
   465	        os << "not C-contiguous (shape " << shape_str(shape_of(t)) << ", strides [";
   466	        for (int j = 0; j < t->ndim; ++j) os << (j ? ", " : "") << t->strides[j];
   467	        os << "]); pass a contiguous tensor";
   468	        refuse_value(role, os.str());
   469	      }
   470	      want *= t->shape[i];
   471	    }
   472	  }
   473	  if (t->data == nullptr) refuse_value(role, "NULL data pointer");
   474	  // F03: data + byte_offset (element 0) and element 0 + nbytes (one past the end) must not wrap the address space.
   475	  uintptr_t p = 0;
   476	  uintptr_t end = 0;
   477	  if (__builtin_add_overflow(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(t->data), static_cast<uintptr_t>(t->byte_offset), &p) ||
   478	      __builtin_add_overflow(p, static_cast<uintptr_t>(nbytes), &end))
   479	    refuse_value(role, "data + byte_offset + byte size overflows the address space");
   480	  if (p % alignof(float) != 0) refuse_value(role, "data + byte_offset is not 4-byte aligned");
   481	  return numel;
   482	}
   483	
   484	void require_operand(py::handle obj, const char* role) {
   485	  // F02 (P1T-FIX1): None is never a tensor. Refused with TypeError before any planning, allocation or consumption.
   486	  if (obj.is_none())
   487	    refuse_type(role, "is required: expected a DLPack producer (an object with __dlpack__) or a DLPack capsule, got "
